<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aung San</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bogyoke Aung San</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aung San was a Burmese nationalist leader who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma-independence-army">Burma Independence Army</a> with Japanese help in 1941. He led the army of the Japanese-sponsored State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> but turned it against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1945. He negotiated independence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and agreed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panglong-conference">Panglong Conference</a> with Shan, Kachin and Chin leaders in 1947, but was assassinated in July 1947, months before independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aung-san.jpg" alt="Aung San" width="300" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aung_San_color_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aung San was born on 13 February 1915 in Natmauk, Magway District, the youngest of nine children of a middle-class family. His father, U Phar, was a lawyer who went into business instead. He entered Rangoon University in 1933 and became a student leader, elected to the executive committee of the Rangoon University Students' Union and editor of its magazine Oway. In February 1936 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-nu">U Nu</a> were expelled for refusing to name the author of an article criticizing a senior university official, which led to the Second University Students' Strike, after which both were reinstated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In October 1938 he left his law classes for national politics and became a Thakin by joining the Dobama Asiayone, acting as its general secretary until August 1940. He helped organise the countrywide strikes known as the ME 1300 Revolution. With the outbreak of war he helped found the Freedom Bloc under Dr. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-maw">Ba Maw</a>. In 1940 he met Nehru, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> assembly in Ramgarh, and then spent the rest of the year in Tokyo.</p><p>On 28 December 1941 he and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-comrades">Thirty Comrades</a> inaugurated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma-independence-army">Burma Independence Army</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, with about 3,500 volunteers. It entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> behind the Japanese Fifteenth Army, and its arrival in many areas was followed by communal violence, especially against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karen-people">Karen people</a>, until the Japanese Army intervened. Aung San served as minister of war under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ba-maw">Ba Maw</a> in the Japan-backed State of Burma, then switched sides and merged his forces with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he signed an agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a> on 27 January 1947 on the terms of independence; U Saw and Thakin Ba Sein refused to sign it, and Karen and Shan leaders warned they would not consider it binding on their communities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At 10:37 on 19 July 1947 a jeep carrying gunmen in military fatigues drove into the courtyard of the Secretariat Building, where Aung San was meeting his cabinet. The gunmen shot the guard, burst into the chamber and shot Aung San in the chest. Four other council members died at once and three more were mortally wounded. U Saw, the last pre-war prime minister, was arrested the same day and later hanged, but other claims of responsibility have been made, including that a rogue faction in British intelligence was involved. Aung San is considered the founder of modern Myanmar and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatmadaw">Tatmadaw</a>, and is called the Father of the Nation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aung San” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Burmese nationalism grew from student and Buddhist movements in the 1920s and 1930s, and young Thakins such as <b>Aung San</b> trained with the Japanese, entered Burma with them in 1942, then turned against them in March 1945.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Independence hero <b>Aung San</b> had been assassinated six months earlier, and civil conflict soon followed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-4">4 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Negotiations with <b>Aung San's</b> Anti-Fascist People's Freedom League led to independence on 4 January 1948 as the Union of Burma.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-burma">British Burma</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Panglong Conference of 1947 Shan leaders agreed with <b>Aung San</b> to join an independent Burma, and the Federated Shan States became Shan State of the Union of Burma in 1948.</blockquote><a href="/state/federated-shan-states">Federated Shan States</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan invaded British Burma in 1942 with the help of the Burma Independence Army, formed by <b>Aung San</b> and the Thirty Comrades.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-burma">State of Burma</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Natmauk, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.35,95.4&amp;year=1935" aria-label="Natmauk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7121 1410 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aung San"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7571 1680h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7571 1680h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=20.35,95.4&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
